Active Directory strictly relies on DNS to function. When a Windows laptop wants to join a domain or authenticate a user, it doesn't automatically know where the Domain Controller (DC) is located. It asks DNS.

The Client Queries DNS:
When an employee logs into their laptop as CORP\chandan, the laptop asks the DNS server: "Where is the Domain Controller for corp.local?"

DNS Resolves via SRV Records:
Active Directory registers special Service Location records (SRV records) inside DNS (such as _ldap._tcp.dc._msdcs.corp.local). DNS replies: "The Domain Controller is at IP 192.168.1.10."

Active Directory Authenticates:
The laptop now reaches out directly to 192.168.1.10 over Port 88 (Kerberos) to verify the user's password and load their profile.

If DNS fails in an enterprise environment, Active Directory breaks completely because clients can no longer locate their Domain Controllers to log in.
